He had watched the semi-final against England in the King Tom Croatia Club with family, friends and the wider Croatian community, but he'll be watching his country take on France in the World Cup final in the famed fan zone in Zagreb's main square. He's not the only one. As Mario Mandzukic secured Hrvatska's first World Cup final appearance with an extra time goal, and the final whistle had sounded – Robert said others knew they had to join him. "I��m telling you they were all walking out of the club and going 'Tonight, this afternoon, tomorrow – I don��t care, I��m going [to Croatia].'" Many of those were headed into the club's adjoining travel agent, where Viktorija was in for one of the busier mornings she's had at Experience Croatia. "[I was] holding my phone to my right ear and I��ve got a headset on my left side," the travel agent said.
"[They came in] still calling their wives telling them they are leaving, but all the women were very very considerate and happy to send them over – everybody is just so happy." Croatian fans in Zagreb during the World Cup semi-final against England. Credit:AP Photo/Nikola Solic Viktorija struggled to sum up the emotions running through her community, but when pressed she came up with two words: "Pure love. Nothing else but pure love." "After I parked there was one man coming in and he was just kissing everybody," she said. Kissing was a bit of a theme on Thursday morning, just ask Robert. "I don��t think I kissed this many people on my wedding day – it��s a good feeling."
The King Tom Club board member had all the emotion in the world swirling as he readied to fly into Zagreb on Friday night, but there was one key factor that stood out as he readied to take the day-long flight. "Creating a memory with my 10-year-old son that loves football. He'll never forget this," he said. "He gets emotional and he cries when we score a goal. When he gets to see what's going on over there, he��s going to flip out – I can��t wait." Cost, on the other hand, was not one of those factors affecting the decision to embark on a "priceless" experience with his son Karlo, who epitomises a childlike love of the game. "At the club we watch the game and there��s 5-600 people there and it gets rowdy.
"I wake up at 1:30 in the morning and my wife is painting my son��s face. He can��t wake up for school at 7:30 in the morning and it's 1:30 and he jumps out of bed like a rocket!" Loading "It��s amazing what a sport like football can do; any nationality," he said. "It's beautiful. A round ball and two goals – one at each end – what it can do to people... it��s magic." Sydney's Croatian community and football have a long history. During the 1980s Sydney Croatia, now Sydney United, were drawing crowds in the region of 10,000 fans while playing in the NSW State League. The national league averaged about 3000 at the time. Sixty-two Socceroos have passed through the club at some stage in their career and you might recognise a few of the names: Tony Popovic, Mark Bosnich, Robbie Slater, Craig Foster, 2018 World Cup captain Mile Jedinak, Tim Cahill... the list goes on.
The club's history is a source of great pride at King Tom Club, where the home ground is still situated, and members still swap stories about the days of old. "I remember being a 13-year-old kid and going to the King Tom Club and the stadium out the back," Robert said. "Your whole lives were organised around soccer – if you had a kid and had your kid's birthday party and you did it when Sydney Croatia were playing … everyone would be 'Hang on a second what are you doing, we��re going to the soccer'. "When Sydney Croatia played you'd go to watch a three o'clock game and your dad would be in the club 'til midnight and I'll be sitting there waiting for him – I've got school tomorrow! That��s what it was like back then." Fans celebrate at King Tom Club in Sydney after the FIFA World Cup semi-final between Croatia and England. Credit:Jeremy Ng
Members are proud of the role they played in so many players' careers when they were still teenagers. Robert will never forget the story another former player, Graham Arnold, told at the club's 60-year anniversary. "His first contract was $12,000. He wanted $15,000 so the president at the club did a deal. 'Nah mate, we��re going to give you $12,000, but we��ll sort you out a new concrete driveway'," Robert recalled. The FIFA World Cup final is the biggest sporting event on the planet. One billion people watched the 2014 edition of the final when Argentina fell short against Germany. But for Robert, the final – win or lose – is just the beginning. "If they lose to France in this final... the party is not going to be any smaller [when they come back] than if they do win{r}."{r}
