AUBURN -- Four reserve players won't be with the Tigers in 2010, head coach Gene Chizik confirmed Saturday.
Linebackers Adam Herring and Spencer Pybus and offensive lineman Vance Smith are out due to injuries. Defensive lineman Cameron Henderson, who was suspended for the entire 2009 season, has left the team and is no longer enrolled at Auburn, Chizik said.
Pybus, a Dothan native, was a freshman All-SEC linebacker in 2008 but missed all of last year after suffering at least one concussion in practice.
"We've had some unfortunate medical issues with a few of our guys," Chizik said. "(Pybus) is a great kid, a great Auburn kid. He's going to get his degree. He's going to be involved with our football team. But right now, he's not going to play."
Herring was thrust into a starting role in 2009 when Eltoro Freeman was out. He underwent surgery on his heel in December of 2008 and may have reaggravated the injury late in 2009. Herring's father Reggie is linebackers coach for the Dallas Cowboys.
Smith started his career as a tight end but later moved to offensive tackle and played only sparingly. His injury is unknown.
"Those are three great kids, who absolutely love football and there's nothing that tears them up more," Chizik said. "But you got look at what your quality of life is going to be like 30 years from now. At the end of the day, that's what you have to measure."
Henderson, a well-regarded prospect from Shades Valley High in the Birmingham area, leaves Auburn without playing a snap. He redshirted in 2008 and was in Chizik's doghouse for unknown reasons in 2009.
In other news, Chizik said no one has requested official permission to interview offensive coordinator Gus Malzahn, who was one of several names mentioned as a possibility at Louisiana Tech. Former Tech coach Derek Dooley was recently hired at Tennessee.
Chizik said he expects to have his entire staff return "intact" and didn't think speculation would affect recruiting.
"I think everybody understands how the rumor mill works and how the different things get out on the Internet sites," he said. "I think we've all been through that. I went through it. There are a lot of things out there, but I think people know they're not necessarily fact."
