I hope that in these final days of summer you are beating the heat as we are preparing to head into the beautiful Oregon Fall season. The changing of the season makes me think about transitions in general and how nonprofits specifically fare during transitions in organizational structure and mission.
The Evergreen Aviation and Space Museum (EASM) recently went through a reorganization that could have led to a museum closure, but instead the organization emerged as stronger and more connected to the community. Ann Witsil, the Interim CEO, and Clarinda White, the Interim Development Director at EASM, are both part of the Nonprofit Association of Oregon (NAO) longstanding Executive Transition Services (ETS) team and led the efforts at the Museum to help rebuild and reshape its legacy. The ETS program at NAO places interim executives into organizations experiencing transitions of leadership or business models and they give expert advice as they lead the organization directly through the transition. EASM is now aligning with different groups in the community to provide a location and forum in which to teach, inform and ignite the imagination and intuition of the next generation.
NAO is proud to be a part of the solution to what could have been a terrible loss of a nationally-recognized museum located right here in Oregon and the loss of 35 full-time and 75 part-time jobs, along with dozens of docents, in the McMinnville area. So what happens when large organizations do cease to operate? News out of New York about the fate of the $250 million human service provider, Federation Employment and Guidance Services (FEGS), serves as a grim reminder of the effects of a nonprofit failure and points to some disturbing trends. FEGS recently filed for bankruptcy after 80 years of operating, leaving 1,900 employees without jobs, and unpaid creditors holding more than $47 million in debt. This news comes from the Human Services Council (HSC), an independent organization hired to analyze how such an event could happen.
According to HSC's report, New York Nonprofits in the Aftermath of FEGS: A Call to Action, "FEGS was funded largely through public contracts with governmental agencies. Over the past 40 years, government has transferred most legally mandated support functions to the more efficient and nimble nonprofit sector at a fraction of what it would cost government to administer directly because of lower expenses at nonprofits and investments made by private funders." The area that nonprofit executives and boards need to be wary of is when they so significantly underbid a project, they are cutting into the "muscle" of their organization's future sustainability.
The HSC goes on to outline the problems that led up to the bankruptcy of FEGS:
Human service nonprofits have a higher rate of insolvency than other types of nonprofits. Organizations with budgets from $10 million to $49 million are most likely to be in financial distress than those but budgets less than $1 million. And, over 60% of these organizations have no more than three months of cash reserves.
The recommended cash reserves amount for nonprofits of any size is six months, according to the Independent Sector's Principles for Good Governance and Ethical Practice.
Government contracts are causing a lot of problems. Low reimbursement rates (typically 80 cents or less of each dollar of true program expense) help create budget gaps no private funder is enthused to fill. In addition, delays in contract payments can force providers to undertake costly borrowing to fulfill direct cost obligations. In a National Study of Nonprofit-Government Contracts and Grants in 2013, the Urban Institute found that 36% of Oregon's nonprofits were reporting problems with late payments. Multiple and redundant audits and complicated reporting requirements, which often accompany government funds, can lead to significant administrative costs that are often not recouped. This chronic underfunding drives down the available funds for nonprofit staff salaries, in some cases forcing the employees themselves to depend on safety net programs like food stamps and Medicaid.
Weak internal financial and programmatic reporting leaves nonprofit boards and executive in the dark. Providers may not be alerted to fiscal dangers early enough to address them.
What can be done to stop large nonprofits from going under and leaving thousands of vulnerable citizens in the lurch? The HCS puts forth recommendations ranging from nonprofits in the development of programs, and improving timeliness of payments, to developing more robust reporting systems which enable performance monitors necessary for long-term sustainability and program quality.
At NAO, we are also concerned with the sustainability of Oregon nonprofits as they strive to fulfill their missions.
